{
  "summary": "The 'exploit existing seams' proposal from round 13 sounds elegant but conflates structural observation with actionable design. Yes, _run_pipeline_impl already has three sequential blocks with stage-check boundaries (lines 1564, 1657, 1694), but 'the seams are already there' is not a design — it's an observation that every prior proposal also made. The proposal fails to specify what mechanism actually stops the pipeline at a seam: without an explicit config field like terminal_stage, there is no runtime signal to halt execution between blocks. The stage checks at lines 1564 and 1657 are entry guards (they check whether the feature is in the right stage to start that block), not exit gates — they don't prevent falling through to the next block. After the plan review loop completes and moves a feature to 'approved' (line 1605), execution falls straight through to the spec-writing block at line 1657 because the feature is now in a stage that triggers spec writing. You cannot 'exploit the seam' without adding a check between blocks, and that check IS terminal_stage by another name. The proposal also inherits the same limitation it criticizes in terminal_stage: it only addresses truncation. Saying 'the routing after each block converges to well-defined handoff stages' is true but irrelevant to the skip-early-phases problem — the handoff stages are outputs of blocks that ran, not entry points for blocks that didn't. Furthermore, the proposal overlooks a practical concern: the spec-writing block (lines 1657-1692) has no stage guard at all — it runs unconditionally after the planning block completes, meaning any 'seam-based' approach would need to add a guard that doesn't currently exist, undermining the claim that no new mechanism is needed. The strongest path remains terminal_stage with an explicit config field, precisely because the 'seams' the proposal identifies are not self-enforcing boundaries — they require exactly the kind of explicit gating that terminal_stage provides."
}
